WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Memorizing versus learning.

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:06.000
Very often people say to me, you
know, I can't learn languages

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.710
because I have a poor memory.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:12.240
And many people think that language
learning is a matter of memorizing things.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.710
Uh, it's not uncommon to see
people studying lists of words.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.635
Um, bookstores have, you know,
the thousand words or the 5,000

00:00:22.635 --> 00:00:24.315
words, you know, for TOEFL.

00:00:24.705 --> 00:00:27.795
Uh, and people study these and they
deliberately try to memorize them.

00:00:28.305 --> 00:00:33.495
Uh, some people have, um, memorization
techniques, uh, some of these

00:00:33.495 --> 00:00:37.935
memorization techniques called mnemonics
a date back to the ancient Romans.

00:00:38.235 --> 00:00:40.635
Um, I don't use any of these.

00:00:40.635 --> 00:00:42.915
I don't even use space
repetition actually.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.190
Uh, I make no deliberate
attempt to memorize anything.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:54.010
To me, learning is more what the
great Brazilian educator Rubem Alves

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:57.210
described as a vagabond experience.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:02.519
What I've sometimes called
grazing, uh, what Robert Bjork

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.410
referred to as interleaving.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:10.110
It's kind of exploring different
things, seeing something over here,

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:15.630
which we then forget and see again
somewhere else, and slowly we learn.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.800
Uh, it is important to
have a positive attitude.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.420
It is important to spend the
time, but it's also important to

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.430
be aware of how the brain works.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.540
Again, I often quote Manfred
Spitzer, he says, the brain

00:01:31.185 --> 00:01:32.774
cannot do otherwise than learn.

00:01:33.464 --> 00:01:39.615
The brain learns slowly and the brain
requires repetition and novelty.

00:01:40.125 --> 00:01:44.895
So with the repetition, it's not a
matter of trying to repeat something to

00:01:44.895 --> 00:01:49.815
yourself over and over again, or reading
the same list over and over again, or

00:01:50.024 --> 00:01:56.685
testing yourself with flashcards, it's
more a matter of noticing things that

00:01:56.685 --> 00:01:59.625
cause certain neurons to fire together.

00:01:59.805 --> 00:02:06.405
So if I read a text in Persian and then
I listen to the same text, the audio of

00:02:06.405 --> 00:02:11.745
it, I will remember having read some of
those words, especially if I'm all already

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:13.665
somewhat familiar with those words.

00:02:14.055 --> 00:02:19.755
And then I hear them and then I notice
them and, and then I might read it again.

00:02:20.325 --> 00:02:25.005
And all of this is causing these
neurons, at least is the way I see it,

00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:29.355
to fire together and gradually those
connections are getting stronger.

00:02:29.805 --> 00:02:34.695
And because the brain requires both
repetition and novelty, if you can

00:02:34.695 --> 00:02:39.990
do this, you know, repeating in
contexts that are of interest to

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.970
you, that contain new information...

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:47.250
like I listened to the news in Persian,
uh, that's gonna help you remember it.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.630
Uh, or conversely, because the
repetition is so important.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:55.710
Uh, you know, in fact, I see this
whole repetition thing as a matter

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.870
of, of hooks, of connections.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.170
I think there's a, an expression in
this Sufi Islamic, uh, philosophy that

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:09.270
says you can only learn things that you
already know, so you have to have seen

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:14.280
it once, and then you discover it again,
or you hear it, and then you read it.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.210
And all of this is helping to
reinforce these neural connections.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:23.070
To enable you to learn words, not
through memorization, but through this

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:27.840
exploring vagabond type of learning.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.420
The same is true if with grammar.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.050
If you have no experience with the
language and you read a grammar

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.870
explanation, it makes no impact on you.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:42.454
If you have had a lot of exposure to the
language and then you read an explanation

00:03:42.454 --> 00:03:47.465
of a structure, it's possible that that
might help you notice that structure.

00:03:47.795 --> 00:03:51.995
Once you've noticed that structure
in different situations, read it and

00:03:51.995 --> 00:03:57.515
heard it, and been aware of it and and
so forth, then because you now sort of

00:03:57.605 --> 00:04:00.545
kind of know it, you can now learn it.

00:04:00.815 --> 00:04:05.095
And that sort of transformation
from something that you have

00:04:05.095 --> 00:04:09.595
subconsciously sort of acquired to
where it becomes something that you

00:04:09.595 --> 00:04:15.054
can consciously use, that is a gradual
process and not one that, in my

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:17.545
opinion, we can deliberately control.

00:04:17.784 --> 00:04:22.284
So I think every effort to deliberately
memorize something or deliberately

00:04:22.284 --> 00:04:24.810
learn something is not very efficient.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:30.570
I don't say that it's useless because it
is a form of exposure, but uh, there's all

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:35.490
kinds of research that shows that if we
read the same material over and over again

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:40.530
with the intention of sort of memorizing
it, we are in fact learning less and less,

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:46.095
and we are better off to provide that
novelty, that change to the brain so that

00:04:46.095 --> 00:04:50.865
the brain can go off and look at something
else and then come back and rediscover,

00:04:50.865 --> 00:04:55.905
relearn something that's gonna be a
more efficient way of acquiring words.

00:04:56.205 --> 00:04:59.745
And as I've said many times,
language learning is largely

00:05:00.075 --> 00:05:02.085
a process of acquiring words.

00:05:02.145 --> 00:05:08.820
So the, the language learning um,
the model that I like is one where

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:14.310
I'm learning in a vagabond way
rather than deliberately memorizing.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:19.380
And I think part of doing that, and part
of, uh, believing in that is, is, is to

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.810
accept the fact that that is how we learn.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:26.310
Don't try to short circuit, speed it
up, force yourself, shove it, shove it

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:27.960
down your throat, it isn't gonna work.

00:05:28.545 --> 00:05:32.685
Believe that your brain is always
learning, and if you give it enough

00:05:32.685 --> 00:05:37.065
exposure in different situations,
uh, some easy content, some difficult

00:05:37.065 --> 00:05:41.835
content, sometimes listening to the
words, sometimes reading the words, maybe

00:05:41.835 --> 00:05:45.885
doing the odd, you know, matching pairs,
which is my favorite form of, of, you

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:49.845
know, flashcard review, but other people
have other forms of flashcard review.

00:05:49.935 --> 00:05:53.775
It's all part of the exposure
to the language and the greater

00:05:53.775 --> 00:05:56.085
the variety of the exposure.

00:05:56.385 --> 00:06:01.125
The greater the different levels
of difficulty, the more we are

00:06:01.125 --> 00:06:05.205
learning and the less we are
deliberately trying to memorize.

00:06:05.415 --> 00:06:09.885
I think in the end we enjoy the
process more and we're more successful.

00:06:10.005 --> 00:06:14.115
So there's my take on
memorization versus learning.

00:06:14.895 --> 00:06:15.795
Look forward to your comments.

